esp32c3: Arbitrate pins between the BSP's own drivers

Two drivers configuring the same pad is not theoretical on this BSP: it has
happened twice.  The I2C driver routes SDA and SCL to GPIO5 and GPIO6 while
a GPIO test was using GPIO4 and GPIO5, and UART1 takes GPIO7 and GPIO10,
which the note listing the free pads still described as free.  Each driver
wrote its own IO_MUX and GPIO matrix registers, the last writer won, and
nothing reported anything.  Under QEMU, where no pads are attached, the
collision has no symptom at all.

The shared GPIO layer already does this bookkeeping in
rtems_gpio_request_pin(), and the I2C and UART drivers deliberately do not
use it: reaching it means linking gpio-support.c, a little over 2000 lines
with a mutex per bank and an optional interrupt server task, into every
application that merely has an I2C bus, on a part with 320 KiB of RAM.

So this adds the smallest thing that catches the collision -- a 22 bit mask
and one owner pointer per pad, claimed under an interrupt lock so it is
callable from driver initialisation before any thread exists.  A driver that
is refused says who holds the pad rather than failing silently or, worse,
succeeding.

The GPIO driver claims as well as the shared layer, because the shared layer
cannot see a pad taken by I2C or UART1.  A GPIO claim is never released:
rtems_gpio_release_pin() keeps its bookkeeping in the shared layer and there
is no rtems_gpio_bsp_release() hook for it to call.  Re-requesting the pad as
GPIO still works, since a re-claim by the same owner succeeds; what is
refused is I2C or UART1 taking a pad GPIO used earlier and has since
released.  That is a false refusal and it is the safe direction to be wrong
in -- the other way round is the silent collision this exists to stop.

